CSS Modules let a component author write ordinary CSS while treating class and animation names as local to the module. The build step transforms those names so two files can use the same source name without unintentionally sharing a selector. That reduces global naming collisions, but it does not make CSS rules inherently correct or remove the cascade. Global styles, resets, design tokens, and deliberate escape hatches still need an explicit architecture. I would keep component styles close to the component, expose only intentional composition points, and avoid making generated class names part of a public API. The exact transformation is tooling-dependent, so the team should document its loader and testing setup.

```css caption="The source selector is authored as a module-local class."
.button {
  color: var(--color-button-text);
}

/* Button.module.css */
```

Utility-first styling composes small classes in the markup instead of inventing a bespoke selector for every component. In current Tailwind, theme variables define design values and can generate corresponding utilities, so the useful boundary is the project theme rather than arbitrary values everywhere. I would keep a constrained token vocabulary, use component abstractions for repeated semantic patterns, and reserve arbitrary values for genuine exceptions. I would also agree on class ordering, responsive conventions, and where custom CSS belongs. Tailwind is a tool, not a design system by itself: accessibility, component APIs, documentation, and visual regression checks still need separate decisions.

```css caption="A Tailwind theme variable creates a reusable project color utility."
@import "tailwindcss";

@theme {
  --color-brand: oklch(0.62 0.19 250);
}
```

CSS custom properties are part of the browser's CSS model: they participate in the cascade, inherit by default, and are resolved where they are used through `var()`. That makes them useful for runtime theming and values that change with the DOM context. A preprocessor variable is normally substituted during build time and is gone from the delivered CSS. I would use custom properties for semantic runtime values such as surface or text colors, with fallbacks where appropriate, and use build-time variables for authoring calculations or configuration. I would name tokens by purpose rather than raw color, and keep the token contract documented.

```css caption="The same component rule consumes a runtime theme token."
:root {
  --color-surface: white;
}

[data-theme=dark] {
  --color-surface: #111;
}

.card { background: var(--color-surface); }
```

I would evaluate the library's rendering model, server-rendering and streaming support, runtime cost, extraction or build-time behavior, browser output, theming model, debugging, and interoperability with consumers. CSS-in-JS can colocate styles and component logic and can make variants expressive, but a runtime solution may add work during rendering and can complicate SSR ordering or CSP. Static CSS, CSS Modules, or native CSS variables may be a better fit when predictable output, broad framework interoperability, or low runtime work matters most. The decision should be measured against the library's actual targets rather than treated as a universal preference. Document the escape hatches and public styling contract.

```tsx caption="A stable attribute contract can keep component semantics independent of the styling engine."
export function Notice({ tone }: { tone: "info" | "warning" }) {
  return <div data-tone={tone} role="status" />;
}
```

Start with the component's behavior and accessibility contract, then expose a small, semantic styling API: variants, sizes, states, slots, and deliberate composition points. Prefer tokens and documented parts over asking consumers to target internal DOM or generated class names. A `className` or style escape hatch can be useful, but define where it applies and how conflicts are resolved. For interaction-heavy components, customization must not break focus management, keyboard behavior, or semantics. I would publish examples for common layouts, test the supported themes and states, and treat changes to the public styling hooks as API changes. This follows the component-design interview expectation to define the external API before implementation details.

```tsx caption="Expose semantic variants rather than internal selector names."
type ButtonProps = {
  variant?: "solid" | "ghost";
  size?: "sm" | "md";
  className?: string;
};
```

I would establish a documented order: reset or base, tokens, primitives, components, utilities, and application composition, with third-party CSS imported into a controlled layer. Keep selectors shallow, avoid `!important` as a routine override strategy, and use component-local scoping where it reduces collisions. Cascade layers help make ordering explicit, but they do not replace understanding origin, specificity, inheritance, or source order. A system should also define ownership: which package owns tokens, which styles are public, and how consumers override them. I would verify the architecture with representative conflicts and visual regression tests, including production bundling and CSS order.

```css caption="Declare the intended layer order and demote vendor styles into a known layer."
@layer reset, tokens, components, utilities, app;

@import "vendor.css" layer(reset);

@layer tokens {
  :root { --space-2: 0.5rem; }
}
```

Use layers of meaning rather than scattering raw values through components. Primitive tokens describe scales; semantic tokens describe roles such as `text.primary`, `surface.canvas`, or `action.accent`; component tokens describe a component-specific contract only when a semantic token is insufficient. Themes should replace values behind the same semantic names, so components do not branch on brand or mode. Keep aliases explicit and validate type, contrast, and fallback behavior. The Design Tokens Community Group format models names, values, types, groups, aliases, and deprecation metadata, which supports exchanging decisions between tools. I would generate platform outputs from the source tokens and review token changes as API changes.

```json caption="A semantic token can alias a primitive token in the DTCG format."
{
  "color": {
    "text": {
      "primary": {
        "$type": "color",
        "$value": "{color.neutral.900}"
      }
    }
  }
}
```

Treat every published contract as part of the API: component props, exported names, token names, CSS custom properties, supported slots, accessibility behavior, and documented styling hooks. Removing or renaming one, changing its type, or changing behavior consumers rely on is breaking and should require a major release under SemVer. New backward-compatible components or options are minor; backward-compatible fixes are patch releases. Deprecate before removal, publish migration guidance, and test representative consumer applications. Visual changes need judgment: a deliberate redesign can be breaking even when TypeScript compiles if it changes layout, contrast, or interaction expectations. Never rewrite an already published version; ship a new version.

```ts caption="Token names are part of the consumer-facing contract when documented and exported."
// A breaking public-token rename requires a major release.
// v2: --color-action-primary
// v3: --color-action-accent
```

Publish the visual language as a versioned package with tokens and documented primitives, not as a copy of each application's generated CSS. Tailwind's current theme-variable model allows shared variables to live in an importable CSS file, and the same variables become regular CSS variables in the compiled output. Consumers can choose the components and utilities they need while receiving a compatible token contract. Keep application-specific composition local, define a support matrix for Tailwind and browser targets, and test the package in more than one consumer. Release token changes with migration notes, deprecations, and visual regression coverage; otherwise a shared stylesheet becomes a hidden cross-application dependency.

```css caption="A shared Tailwind theme can be imported by multiple applications."
/* packages/brand/theme.css */
@theme {
  --color-brand: oklch(0.62 0.19 250);
}

/* app.css */
@import "tailwindcss";
@import "@company/brand/theme.css";
```

Make it incremental and contract-driven. First inventory the public styling surface, theme values, SSR behavior, and places where consumers target generated output. Freeze new dependencies on the old runtime, then introduce a stable token layer using CSS custom properties and a replacement component or primitive boundary. Migrate low-risk components first, compare server and client output, and use visual regression tests for each state and theme. Keep adapters where necessary so product teams can move independently, and publish deprecation guidance rather than changing every call site at once. Measure bundle and render effects in representative routes, because migration success is both behavioral correctness and operational improvement.

```tsx caption="A stable component boundary allows the underlying styling implementation to change."
export function Card({ children }: { children: React.ReactNode }) {
  return <section className="card">{children}</section>;
}
```

I would define a product, not only a component repository. Establish ownership and contribution paths, a public API and support policy, design and code review responsibilities, accessibility and visual-quality gates, release and deprecation policy, and a feedback loop with consuming teams. Separate foundations such as tokens from components and application composition, and publish compatibility guidance for frameworks and browsers. Measure adoption and outcomes—such as reduced duplication, issue resolution, upgrade success, and accessibility regressions—rather than counting components. The technical system should make the safe path easy: documented examples, stable packages, automated visual and accessibility checks, migration tooling, and clear escape hatches for cases the system does not serve.

```ts caption="A design system should make its consumer contract and deprecation path explicit."
type ChangePolicy = {
  publicApi: string[];
  deprecatedSince?: string;
  removalTarget?: string;
};
```
